Abstract = {Aim: To describe the effect of introducing the CISOE-A framework for
   reporting cervical cytology results, including changes in repeat and
   referral advice in the Netherlands, on the efficacy of the screening
   programme. Changes in the distribution of cytological results, the
   detection rate of cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) lesions, and
   the detection rate of squamous cervical carcinoma are reported.
   Methods: The results of all gynaecology cytological and histological
   examinations, as registered in the nationwide database for
   histopathology and cytopathology (PALGA) from 1990 to 2000, were
   retrieved from seven laboratories in the greater Amsterdam area.
   Results: After the introduction of the CISOE-A classification,
   cytological results with equivocal diagnoses decreased significantly
   from 11.3\% to 2.6\%, without an increase in the percentages of moderate
   dyskaryosis or worse. During the study period, the detection rate of
   histologically diagnosed high grade CIN lesions increased significantly
   from 4.1 to 6.4/1000 smears, whereas there was no change in the
   detection rates of low grade lesions or invasive cervical cancer.
   Conclusions: The introduction of the new CISOE-A classification system
   resulted in a substantial decrease of equivocal results and repeat
   recommendations, without a decrease in the detection rate of high grade
   lesions, making the screening programme more efficacious.},
